To show project reports using the Apache Maven Site Plugin, you configure reporting plugins within a dedicated <reporting> XML block inside your pom.xml.The most common example for a standard Core Java application utilizes the Maven Surefire Report Plugin to display test results and the Maven Project Info Reports Plugin to display basic project specifications.1. Configure the pom.xmlAdd the plugins into the separate <build> and <reporting> sections of your pom.xml file:xml<project xmlns="http://apache.org"
         xmlns:xsi="http://w3.org"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://apache.org http://apache.org">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>core-java-reports</artifactId>
    <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>

    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.source>17</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>17</maven.compiler.target>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- JUnit 5 for Core Java Testing -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter-api</artifactId>
            <version>5.11.0</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!-- Core plugin to run tests -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.5.0</version>
            </plugin>
            <!-- Core plugin to build the HTML site structure -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-site-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.21.0</version>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

    <!-- This section registers reports hooked into the "mvn site" command -->
    <reporting>
        <plugins>
            <!-- Generates Project Metadata reports (Dependencies, Info, etc.) -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-project-info-reports-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.6.0</version>
            </plugin>
            <!-- Generates Unit Test execution reports -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-report-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.5.0</version>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </reporting>
</project>
Use code with caution.2. Add a Sample Core Java TestCreate a basic unit test file to ensure the reporting engine has real test logs to parse. Place this file under src/test/java/com/example/AppTest.java:javapackage com.example;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertTrue;

public class AppTest {
    @Test
    public void shouldPass() {
        assertTrue(true);
    }
}
