The primary difference between a plain Maven site and a Maven site with reporting lies in what type of content is generated into the static website. While a plain Maven site only contains handwritten static documentation, adding a reporting configuration automatically populates the site with dynamic, auto-generated project metrics and code analysis.Here is a direct breakdown of how they compare.Core Differences OverviewFeaturePlain Maven SiteMaven Site with ReportingPrimary GoalDisplays static documentation.Displays static docs plus automated project audits.POM Section UsedConfigured via <build> plugins.Configured via the <reporting> block.Content SourceCustom Markdown, APT, or FML files.Generated programmatically from code, tests, and POM data.Setup ComplexityLow (requires a basic site.xml).Medium (requires linking report plugins).Plain Maven SiteA plain Maven site relies entirely on the Apache Maven Site Plugin to bundle your custom, handwritten documentation.How it works: You create documentation files inside the src/site/ directory using formats like Markdown or APT (Almost Plain Text). You structure the navigation menu using a site.xml file.What you get: A clean, branded static HTML website containing your custom guides, architecture overviews, and FAQs.Execution command:bashmvn site
Use code with caution.Maven Site with ReportingA Maven site with reporting takes the base site structure and injects automated quality, status, and health metrics directly into the left-hand navigation menu.How it works: You add a explicit <reporting> section to your pom.xml. When the site building lifecycle triggers, Maven executes these report plugins to analyze your source code and test directories.What you get: The base site plus an extra "Project Reports" section featuring:Project Info: Dependencies, mailing lists, and team members.Code Quality: Reports from tools like Checkstyle, PMD, or SpotBugs.Testing: Test coverage graphs (JaCoCo/Cobertura) and test failure data (Surefire).API Docs: Integrated Javadocs.Example Reporting ConfigurationTo add reports, you declare your chosen tools inside the <reporting> tag of your pom.xml:xml<reporting>
    <plugins>
        <!-- Generates standard project information (Dependencies, License, etc.) -->
        <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-project-info-reports-plugin</artifactId>
            <version>3.5.0</version>
        </plugin>
        <!-- Automatically embeds Javadocs into the site -->
        <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
            <version>3.6.3</version>
        </plugin>
    </plugins>
</reporting>
Use code with caution.When you execute mvn site with this configuration, Maven hooks into the lifecycle, processes the plugins, and places the resulting HTML reports straight into your target/site/ directory alongside your handwritten text.To help tailor this to your workflow, are you trying to minimize build times on a CI/CD server, or are you looking to generate specific code quality metrics (like test coverage or Javadocs) for your team?